WEBVTT

00:02.350 --> 00:06.090
Visual tutorials show us how
something should be done.

00:06.350 --> 00:08.469
But they are usually made somewhere else,

00:08.509 --> 00:12.737
with someone else's tools,
workspace, and point of view.

00:13.550 --> 00:17.806
So when we follow them, we do
more than follow instructions.

00:17.846 --> 00:23.188
We first have to translate what we see
into the physical situation in front of us.

00:23.950 --> 00:27.504
What if the tutorial started
from your world instead?

00:27.550 --> 00:29.507
What if it could show what should happen next,

00:29.547 --> 00:32.080
right where the task is unfolding?

00:35.150 --> 00:39.661
Generative tutorials are live,
contextualized visual instructions,

00:39.701 --> 00:42.605
generated from the learner's own environment.

00:43.950 --> 00:48.526
Generative Tutorial runs as a
loop on a mixed-reality headset.

00:48.750 --> 00:51.276
First, it models the environment.

00:51.316 --> 00:55.304
From what the headset sees,
it describes the objects in front of you,

00:55.344 --> 00:57.784
their states, and how they relate.

00:58.250 --> 01:01.461
Then it grounds each step
of the task in that model:

01:01.501 --> 01:06.913
the objects involved, and what must
already be true before the step can begin.

01:07.350 --> 01:10.027
Finally, it generates the instruction:

01:10.067 --> 01:14.508
a goal image of your own workspace after
the step, with the changes highlighted,

01:14.548 --> 01:18.474
and a short video demonstrating
the motion in the same scene.

01:19.150 --> 01:21.246
Richer media take longer to make.

01:21.286 --> 01:24.404
Text and spatial cues are ready in seconds;

01:24.444 --> 01:28.881
a goal image takes about fifteen,
and a video about forty-five.

01:29.350 --> 01:33.188
So guidance for the next steps is
made while you work on this one:

01:33.228 --> 01:36.357
from captured context where
the headset can see it,

01:36.397 --> 01:38.970
and from predicted context where it must,

01:39.010 --> 01:43.586
an image of how your counter will
look once the current step is done.

01:43.950 --> 01:47.086
Every instruction should
arrive before you need it.

01:48.150 --> 01:52.726
Here is the whole loop, making a strawberry
matcha latte, seen through the headset.

01:53.350 --> 02:06.710
[Headset] For this, you will need the matcha powder, the small sieve, the bowl, the bamboo whisk, the hot water, the milk, the strawberries, the spoon, the ice, and the tall serving glass.

02:06.750 --> 02:09.510
[Headset] Everything you need is already here.

02:09.550 --> 02:12.640
[Headset] Only the ice is
missing. Grab it from the fridge.

02:13.350 --> 02:15.342
It found everything the recipe needs,

02:15.382 --> 02:18.808
and pointed to the one
ingredient that was missing.

02:21.150 --> 02:24.190
[Headset] Sift the matcha over the empty bowl.

02:24.550 --> 02:26.820
Each step opens with its goal:

02:26.860 --> 02:31.402
your own counter, a few moments from
now, with the changes highlighted.

02:31.950 --> 02:36.433
Then the motion that gets you there,
generated in the same scene.

02:43.150 --> 02:45.497
Fine movements can be slowed down,

02:46.350 --> 02:51.855
and when a detail matters, a close-up
is added, here from above the glass.

02:53.550 --> 02:57.104
Meanwhile, the next step
is already being prepared.

03:06.550 --> 03:09.826
One step at a time, the latte comes together.

03:13.350 --> 03:17.230
[Headset] All workspaces done. Beautiful work.

03:28.950 --> 03:32.219
The same loop builds guidance
from whatever is in front of you:

03:32.259 --> 03:36.034
a bouquet, red-bean mochi,
a table set for dinner.

03:37.350 --> 03:40.607
And it reaches past tasks with one right answer,

03:40.647 --> 03:45.130
to open ones, with no fixed
goal, only a place to start.

03:52.950 --> 03:57.299
We compared generated and pre-authored
guidance in the same interface,

03:57.339 --> 04:00.712
with twenty-four participants
and four everyday tasks.

04:00.752 --> 04:04.028
Everyone used both, in counterbalanced order.

04:04.750 --> 04:07.961
With generated guidance,
task quality was higher,

04:08.001 --> 04:11.072
people confirmed finished
steps about a second sooner,

04:11.112 --> 04:15.252
and they rated the guidance as a
much closer match to their workspace.

04:15.292 --> 04:18.428
Total time and workload were similar.

04:18.950 --> 04:21.453
Generated guidance can also be wrong:

04:21.493 --> 04:25.760
an extra bowl, or five balls of
dough where the text says four.

04:25.800 --> 04:28.395
Because everything else matched their table,

04:28.435 --> 04:33.325
participants had to decide what was an
instruction, and what was an artifact.

04:40.550 --> 04:43.176
Tutorials that start from your world.

04:45.950 --> 04:47.477
Generative Tutorial.

04:47.517 --> 04:52.059
Towards live, contextualized visual
instructions for physical tasks.
